/* INFO5995 — quiz bank.
 * Entry shape: { ch, type: "study"|"math", q, opts: [..], answer: <index>, exp }
 */
var ALL_QUIZ_DATA = [

  /* Ch1 */
  { ch: 1, type: "study", q: "A bank has excellent network monitoring, hardened servers and full-disk encryption, but help-desk staff reset passwords after a phone call with no identity verification. Which reading matches the Week 1 argument?", opts: ["The weakest link sets the practical security level, so the password-reset process is the priority", "Technical controls outweigh process controls, so the reset process does not affect the risk picture", "Defence in depth means every layer is attacked with equal probability, so no layer is a priority", "Password resets are an administrative matter and outside the security lifecycle"], answer: 0, exp: "Week 1's second rule is that a system is only as secure as its weakest point, so effort belongs on the shortest board rather than the ones already tall. The claim that technical controls outweigh process controls inverts the example, which shows a process gap defeating strong technical layers, and equal attack probability per layer is not what defence in depth asserts." },
  { ch: 1, type: "study", q: "In the Bybit case the attackers never read the cold wallet's secrets. They persuaded staff to approve a transaction that was not what it appeared to be. Which property failed, and why?", opts: ["Confidentiality, because the wallet's key material was exposed to the attackers", "Integrity — the approval and the transaction details were falsified before they were trusted", "Availability, because the funds were no longer available to the exchange", "Authenticity of the network path, because the approval screen was served over an untrusted link"], answer: 1, exp: "The authorisation data was altered and then trusted, which is an integrity failure of the approval rather than a disclosure of secrets. Confidentiality is the wrong property because nothing was read that should have been hidden, and availability describes the consequence for the exchange rather than the mechanism that was defeated." },
  { ch: 1, type: "study", q: "A clinic's records server is patched promptly so vulnerability is low, but a leak would be extremely damaging, and a ransomware crew is actively targeting clinics in the city. Using risk as a product of threat, vulnerability and impact, what should the assessment conclude?", opts: ["Risk is low, because one factor being small is enough to make the product small", "Risk is negligible, because all three factors must be high at the same time", "Risk is material, so prevention must be paired with detection, tested backups and recovery", "Risk cannot be assessed without a numerical asset valuation first"], answer: 2, exp: "Threat and impact are both high, so the product stays material even with low vulnerability, and the right response pairs prevention with detection and recovery. Treating one small factor as sufficient misreads the product model, and requiring all three to be high contradicts the gate behaviour that makes the model useful." },
  { ch: 1, type: "study", q: "A threat model is written for an ATM that names only the skimmer and the shoulder surfer. Adding organised crime as an actor changes the model in what way?", opts: ["It raises the impact factor but leaves threat and vulnerability unchanged", "It raises the capability assumption, so controls must be argued against funded tooling, persistence and supply-chain access", "It only adds physical attacks, since organised groups work on the street", "It changes the asset list, because organised crime targets different assets"], answer: 1, exp: "Threat actors are distinguished by goal and capability, and a better-funded actor raises the capability side of the threat term, which changes which controls can be assumed to hold. Impact and the asset list are properties of the system rather than of the actor, so naming a new adversary does not by itself change them." },
  { ch: 1, type: "study", q: "A hospital cannot patch a legacy imaging server, so it isolates the server on its own network segment and adds extra monitoring around it. Which control classification is most precise?", opts: ["A purely preventive technical control that removes the vulnerability", "A compensating control supported by detective monitoring, with residual risk knowingly accepted", "A recovery control, because the hospital can rebuild the server after an incident", "A deterrent control, because the isolated segment discourages attackers"], answer: 1, exp: "The vulnerability remains, so the segment and the monitoring stand in for the patch that cannot be applied and the residual risk is accepted with eyes open. Calling it preventive overstates it because the underlying weakness survives, and rebuild capability is contingency planning rather than what the segment provides." },
  { ch: 1, type: "study", q: "Which NIST Cybersecurity Framework function answers the question: how is risk decided, owned and overseen?", opts: ["Identify, because it inventories assets and assesses risk", "Protect, because it applies access control and training", "Govern, because it covers risk strategy, roles, policy and supply-chain risk management", "Recover, because it restores services and improves them afterwards"], answer: 2, exp: "Govern is the function that establishes and monitors the organisation's risk management strategy, expectations and policy, including roles and supply-chain risk. Identify answers what we have and what matters, while Protect and Recover answer how success is made less likely and how the organisation gets back afterwards." },
  { ch: 1, type: "study", q: "A monitoring system records every change to a medical record, but a clinician can still edit a record without any approval step. Which reading of the Foundations page is correct?", opts: ["Integrity is fully satisfied, because every change is logged and attributable", "Integrity is only partly satisfied: changes are attributable but not authorised", "Integrity is irrelevant once the record is protected as confidential personal information", "Integrity is satisfied if the log is append-only, regardless of the approval step"], answer: 1, exp: "The formal statement requires a change to be both authorised and logged, so attribution without authorisation satisfies only half of it. An append-only log protects the evidence rather than the write path, and confidentiality is a separate property that does not substitute for integrity." },
  { ch: 1, type: "math", q: "An asset worth $80,000 would lose 25% of its value in one successful attack, which currently succeeds about 0.2 times a year. What is the annualised loss expectancy?", opts: ["$20,000 per year", "$4,000 per year", "$16,000 per year", "$80,000 per year"], answer: 1, exp: "Single loss expectancy is 80,000 x 0.25 = $20,000, and the annualised figure multiplies that by the annualised rate of occurrence, 0.2, giving $4,000. The $20,000 distractor stops at the single-event loss, $16,000 would come from applying only the rate to the asset value, and $80,000 treats the whole asset as lost every year." },
  { ch: 1, type: "math", q: "A detection rule catches 99% of real attacks and has a 2% false-positive rate. In a system where 1 event in 1,000 is a real attack, roughly what fraction of alerts are real?", opts: ["About 5%, because the tiny base rate keeps most alerts false", "About 99%, because the detection rate is 99%", "About 2%, because the false-positive rate is 2%", "About 50%, because the true and false positives are comparable"], answer: 0, exp: "Bayes gives 0.99 x 0.001 divided by (0.99 x 0.001 + 0.02 x 0.999), which is about 4.7%, so precision is dominated by the base rate rather than by sensitivity. Quoting the detection rate as the precision is the base-rate fallacy, and 2% confuses the false-positive rate with the share of the alert queue." },
  { ch: 1, type: "math", q: "Which secret carries the most entropy, and by what margin over the weakest?", opts: ["A 4-digit PIN, because digits are hard to guess in order", "An 8-character random lowercase password, because length and alphabet both help", "A 6-word passphrase from a 7776-word list, at about 77.5 bits against about 13.3 bits for the PIN", "They are within a few bits of each other, because all are randomly generated"], answer: 2, exp: "Entropy is log2 of the keyspace: 10^4 gives about 13.3 bits, 26^8 gives about 37.6 bits and 7776^6 gives about 77.5 bits, so the spread is tens of bits rather than a few. Treating all randomly generated secrets as equivalent ignores the size of the space each is drawn from." },

  /* Ch2 */
  { ch: 2, type: "study", q: "A model reports a critical vulnerability with a fluent explanation but no file, line, excerpt or trace. How should this be classified?", opts: ["A confirmed finding, because the mechanism it describes is plausible for this codebase", "An unresolved finding, because the model lacked authorisation to test it", "Hallucination: it sounds correct but is not grounded, so the evidence term is empty", "A refuted finding, because no exploit was demonstrated"], answer: 2, exp: "A finding is a location, a mechanism and evidence, so with no evidence it is a hypothesis rather than a vulnerability, which is exactly what hallucination names. It is not refuted, because nothing has been shown to be false, and not unresolved, because the missing element is the evidence rather than authorisation or an unavailable dependency." },
  { ch: 2, type: "study", q: "Why is reviewing decompiled machine code harder for a language model than reviewing the original source?", opts: ["There is less natural-language and structural signal, fewer clean paired examples, and the code is low-level and ambiguous", "Decompiled output is always far larger than the source it came from", "Decompilers cannot recover enough code to analyse at all", "Machine code has no patterns for a model to recognise"], answer: 0, exp: "The deck's difficulty table ranks artefacts by the signal available: source code has abundant natural-language-like structure and clean paired examples, while machine code is low-level and ambiguous so the model must reason about semantics. Size is not the stated reason, and both the claim that decompilers recover nothing and the claim that machine code has no patterns overstate the case." },
  { ch: 2, type: "study", q: "A team must cover a large codebase with a mix of techniques. Which allocation matches the week's account of each technique's contribution?", opts: ["Fuzzing for whole-codebase rule coverage, static analysis for intent and design judgement", "The language model for dynamic proof, manual review for pattern recognition at volume", "Static analysis for deterministic rule coverage with locations, fuzzing for dynamic proof from a triggering input, and the model for pattern recognition at volume", "The language model for everything, with a human reading its output as the only other step"], answer: 0, exp: "Static analysis supplies deterministic, reproducible findings with file and line; fuzzing supplies a crash that is dynamic evidence; and the model reads unfamiliar code at volume, with its output treated as hypothesis until a human confirms it. Swapping those roles, or asking one technique to do the whole job, is the design mistake the week warns against." },
  { ch: 2, type: "study", q: "The same prompt over the same file returns two different vulnerability lists. What is the correct reading, and what follows for method?", opts: ["The difference proves the file is not the artefact being analysed", "The second run is more accurate because it reasoned for longer", "The model learned from the first response and revised its answer", "Sampling is not deterministic, so the prompt, model and settings belong in the record and repeated runs are a coverage technique"], answer: 0, exp: "A model predicts patterns rather than retrieving a stored answer, so identical requests can differ, and the week turns that from a defect into coverage by re-running with different phrasing, models and temperatures. Treating a later run as automatically more accurate, or as evidence about the file, mistakes sampling variability for learning." },
  { ch: 2, type: "study", q: "Which instruction most improves a shallow analysis of a partial code listing?", opts: ["Supply the missing context: the language, the framework, the surrounding module and the contract the code must satisfy", "Ask the model to state a confidence level for each claim", "Raise the reasoning effort and re-run the same partial listing", "Ask the model to produce its list more concisely"], answer: 0, exp: "Partial code produces shallow analysis, so the remedy is context: the artefact, its build files and the contract it must satisfy. A confidence figure is uncalibrated without evidence, and more effort over the same fragment still samples the surface the deck describes." },
  { ch: 2, type: "study", q: "At 8 a.m. a valid staff login succeeds; by 8:17 bulk student records have been read. Which Week 2 reading of the investigation applies?", opts: ["The report should list only the confirmed findings so the reader is not distracted", "Refuted candidates should be deleted, since they were never vulnerabilities", "The finding must be confirmed, refuted or left unresolved with the reason recorded in each case", "An unresolved candidate is the same as a confirmed one until someone disproves it"], answer: 2, exp: "Verification has three outcomes and all three are results: confirmed with evidence and impact, refuted with the reason so nobody re-chases it, and unresolved with the precise limit stated. Discarding refutations loses the record that stops a false lead being chased twice, and treating an unresolved candidate as confirmed is exactly the unverified claim the week refuses." },
  { ch: 2, type: "math", q: "A 400,000-token repository is reviewed in units of 8,000 tokens. How many units are there, and what does that number not tell you?", opts: ["50 units, and the count says nothing about whether cross-module data paths survive the unit boundary", "32 units, and the count proves the review is complete", "3,200 units, and the count shows the context window is large enough", "50 units, and the count guarantees each unit is independently analysable"], answer: 0, exp: "The unit count is the ceiling of 400,000 divided by 8,000, which is 50, and its limit is that a unit boundary cuts call chains, so cross-module paths are exactly what slicing misses. Dividing the other way gives the meaningless 3,200, and completion or independence are claims the count cannot support." },
  { ch: 2, type: "math", q: "A tool reports 200 candidates at precision 0.25, and each candidate costs 4 minutes to confirm or refute. Roughly how much human time goes on findings that are not real?", opts: ["About 600 minutes: 150 false positives at 4 minutes each", "About 200 minutes, the cost of confirming the 50 real findings", "About 800 minutes, because every candidate must be checked at equal cost", "About 50 minutes, since only the real findings need evidence"], answer: 0, exp: "Precision 0.25 means 50 of the 200 candidates are real, so 150 are not, and 150 x 4 minutes is 600 minutes of refutation. The 800-minute figure is the whole screening bill rather than the wasted part, and 200 minutes describes the work on the genuine findings." },
  { ch: 2, type: "math", q: "A fuzzing campaign reports 90% statement coverage and 41% branch coverage after two hours, with no new crashes in the last 40 minutes. What is the best interpretation?", opts: ["Many conditional paths are never exercised, and the flat stretch cannot be distinguished from a plateau without changing the corpus, mutation strategy or harness", "The campaign is essentially complete, because statement coverage is high", "No crashes in 40 minutes proves the unexercised branches contain no faults", "Statement coverage is the stronger measure, so branch coverage can be ignored"], answer: 0, exp: "The gap between statement and branch coverage marks the guarded paths where interesting faults live, and a flat stretch is only evidence of a plateau once the seeds, mutation strategy or harness have been varied. High statement coverage is a proxy rather than a verdict, and the absence of a crash in a short window says nothing about branches that were never reached." },
  { ch: 2, type: "math", q: "A first pass returns 200 candidates at 4 minutes of triage each, and a filter is added that lets a candidate with no trace be dismissed in seconds instead of being traced by hand. Which term of the cost model does the filter act on, and why?", opts: ["Recall, because the filter removes candidates the tool would otherwise have reported", "The missed-finding cost, because dismissed candidates might have been real", "The per-candidate verification cost, because the filter makes fabricated candidates cheap to reject", "The number of passes, because filtering reduces how often the review must be re-run"], answer: 2, exp: "The countermeasures are requests that make a claim checkable, which shrinks the per-candidate figure that the false-positive count multiplies. Recall and the cost of a miss are properties of what the tool failed to report, which a rejection filter does not touch." },

  /* Ch3 */
  { ch: 3, type: "study", q: "A team can review only one mobile platform deeply. On what grounds does Week 3 justify choosing Android?", opts: ["Because it holds roughly 70% of the market and its architecture is open enough to inspect", "Every line of Android on a shipping phone is open source and therefore auditable", "Because the iOS attack surface has been exhausted by previous research", "Because Android apps cannot be reverse engineered, so analysis stays defensive"], answer: 0, exp: "Market share sets where attacker effort goes and an inspectable architecture sets which controls can be reasoned about, which is the pairing the deck gives. A shipping phone is not the open-source core: vendors add proprietary drivers and licensed services, so the claim that every line is auditable is false, and applications on either platform can be decompiled." },
  { ch: 3, type: "study", q: "Why is a compromised system app a bigger problem than a compromised ordinary app?", opts: ["Because system apps are installed on more devices and so spread further", "Because system apps run outside the sandbox and outside the permission model", "Because a privileged app can reach more, so the same class of mistake causes more harm", "Because system apps cannot be updated once they ship"], answer: 2, exp: "The trust table ranks layers by what a compromise there buys an attacker, and a system app sits higher so its blast radius is larger. Privileged apps are still sandboxed and permission-gated, which is why the deck frames this as consequence rather than exemption." },
  { ch: 3, type: "study", q: "A banking app's transfer screen opens correctly from a link, but the app never verifies that the person tapping the link is the logged-in account holder. Which band of the Week 3 classification does this belong to?", opts: ["Runtime attack, because the failure appears while the software is executing", "Hardware attack, because the request arrived from outside the device", "A remote denial of service, because the screen becomes unreachable", "Gray zone: legitimate access is being used to produce an unintended effect"], answer: 3, exp: "Nothing corrupted memory and no permission was escalated; an access path the app already had is being used to reach an effect the user never agreed to, which is the gray-zone definition. A runtime attack requires crafted input reaching a bug in executing code, and hardware attacks begin with physical contact." },
  { ch: 3, type: "study", q: "A team hides an API key by splitting it across three string constants and reassembling it at runtime. What is the correct assessment?", opts: ["The key is safe, because decompilers cannot reconstruct values built at runtime", "The key is safe as long as the APK signature verifies at install time", "The key is recoverable, because the code that assembles it ships to the device, so the secret belongs on a trusted server", "The key is safe because it never appears as a single literal in the APK"], answer: 2, exp: "If code is delivered to a user's device, someone can inspect it, and the instruction flow that reassembles the secret is part of the delivery. Signature verification proves the package was not altered after signing; it says nothing about who can read it." },
  { ch: 3, type: "study", q: "Which statement about an activity is correct?", opts: ["An activity is a screen layout plus the code that controls it, declared in the manifest", "An activity is only a screen layout, so its security depends on how the layout looks", "An activity is the mechanism Android uses to share data between apps", "An activity is background work with no visible screen and no user interaction"], answer: 0, exp: "The deck's SafeCampus walkthrough pairs activity_login.xml with Login.java and notes that the manifest declares which activities the app can start, which is why a screen is where user intent and attacker intent meet. Data sharing between apps is the content provider's job, and background work is a service." },
  { ch: 3, type: "study", q: "An analyst has one hour with an unfamiliar APK and the source is unavailable. Where does the deck's analysis order say to begin?", opts: ["In res/layout, to see every screen the app can display", "In META-INF, to confirm the signing certificate before anything else", "By decompiling classes.dex and reading the entry point first", "In AndroidManifest.xml, to establish what exists, what starts first, what is requested and what is exposed"], answer: 3, exp: "The manifest answers the four structural questions and tells you which components are reachable before any code is read, which is why the deck's instruction is blunt about opening it first. Layouts and signing material are part of the tour but neither establishes what is public, and reading the decompiled code first means reading paths you have not yet learned are reachable." },
  { ch: 3, type: "study", q: "A manifest exports TransferActivity with MAIN, LAUNCHER and a browsable custom scheme, and the activity reads an amount and a destination account from the intent. What is the most defensible reading?", opts: ["Nothing is wrong, because an intent filter is required for a launcher activity", "The finding is the export flag alone, and the data scheme is irrelevant to it", "The activity is safe if it validates the amount, whatever the session state", "The browsable scheme makes the parameters attacker-controlled, so the screen must not act on them without validation, an authenticated session and confirmation"], answer: 3, exp: "A deep link is an input channel anyone can write to, so the extras are untrusted input and the checks that matter are validation, session authorisation and confirmation of the sensitive action. Validating quantity alone leaves the authorisation question open, and treating the export flag as the whole finding ignores the parameters that reach business logic." },
  { ch: 3, type: "math", q: "During a review, ViewProfile is the only exported component and it renders a record with no session check at all. Which risk statement is most defensible?", opts: ["The risk is maximal, because any exported component is a vulnerability by definition", "The risk is only a runtime concern, because managed code cannot corrupt memory", "The risk is acceptable because the component was not the launcher activity", "Threat is broad, the control is missing and impact is whatever the record discloses, so the risk is real and the fix is a session check or dropping the export"], answer: 3, exp: "The surface sum requires all three terms - reachable from outside, no authorisation, and something that matters - and all three hold here, which is what makes it a finding rather than a keyword match. Export alone is normal for a launcher activity, and the absence of memory corruption is irrelevant to an authorisation gap." },
  { ch: 3, type: "math", q: "A report says that because the sandbox isolates every app, an app's dex code is effectively secret from other apps and from users. What is wrong with the reasoning?", opts: ["Nothing - the sandbox prevents other apps from reading the package", "The sandbox does prevent decompilation, but only for apps installed from a store", "The sandbox bounds what other apps may reach at runtime, but it does not prevent decompilation of the package that ships to the device", "The reasoning fails only because signature verification is not enforced"], answer: 2, exp: "Isolation is a runtime policy over files and processes, while decompilation operates on the package that was delivered to the device, which is why secrets belong on a trusted server. Store installation changes provenance, not readability, and signature verification establishes integrity of the package rather than confidentiality of its contents." },
  { ch: 3, type: "math", q: "An app requests photo-library access so that the user can pick a single image to crop. Which assessment matches the Week 3 model?", opts: ["It is safe, because the user granted the permission explicitly", "It is safe, because photo access is never a sensitive capability", "It is a runtime attack, because the app will process untrusted image data", "The grant is legitimate but the scope is not proportionate: the feature needs one image, not the whole album"], answer: 3, exp: "The gray-zone question survives every grant: was the access necessary, proportionate and used responsibly? A grant is authorisation to ask, not evidence of minimal scope, and processing an image file is not the same as a memory-safety attack." },
  { ch: 3, type: "math", q: "An analyst must inspect an APK with no source and no documentation, and must decide what to read. Which order follows the deck's artefact-to-finding workflow?", opts: ["Manifest for what exists and what is exposed, intent filters for what will match, then dex for what the reachable code paths do", "Dex first for the application logic, then the manifest to confirm the components it references", "Resources first for the screens, then the signing material, then the dex", "Signing material first, because an unexpected signer stops the analysis"], answer: 0, exp: "Configuration first, then code: the manifest establishes the inventory and exposure, the filters establish which external messages reach it, and the decompiled code establishes behaviour on those reachable paths. Starting from the logic means auditing paths that no external caller can reach, and the signing material answers a different question about provenance and intactness." },

  /* Ch4 */
  { ch: 4, type: "study", q: "A team publishes an installer together with its SHA-256 digest on the same web server. Which guarantee have they actually delivered?", opts: ["Integrity against an attacker who can modify the server", "Confidentiality, because the digest does not reveal the installer's contents", "Detection of accidental corruption only, since an active attacker replaces the installer and its digest together", "Nothing, because a digest cannot detect a single changed byte"], answer: 2, exp: "A bare hash is an unkeyed fingerprint: anyone can recompute it for a modified file, so integrity against an active attacker needs a MAC, a signature or an AEAD tag. Hashing is not encryption and hides nothing, and avalanche behaviour actually makes a single changed byte detectable." },
  { ch: 4, type: "study", q: "A vendor argues that its file-encryption product is safe because the scrambling routine is proprietary and undocumented. What is the correct assessment under Kerckhoffs's principle?", opts: ["It is safe, because an attacker cannot read the design that would be needed to break it", "Proprietary designs are always weaker, so publishing the source code is the only fix", "Its security rests on the design staying hidden, so it fails as soon as the design leaks and it never received the public review that built trust in AES", "It is acceptable provided the key is also kept secret"], answer: 2, exp: "The principle says security should rest on the key rather than on the obscurity of the mechanism, so a design whose safety depends on secrecy of the design has a single point of failure and no review history. Publishing source is not what the principle requires, and a strong key does not compensate for a mechanism that fails once it is known." },
  { ch: 4, type: "study", q: "A developer concludes that because AES is the standard cipher, naming it in the design is enough to declare the data safe. What is missing?", opts: ["The mode of operation, the nonce or IV strategy and the authentication mechanism - a cipher name alone does not define the construction", "Only the key length, because AES-128 should be upgraded to AES-256", "Only a random nonce, since the mode is implied by the cipher", "Nothing, because AES is approved by a standard and standards define the construction"], answer: 0, exp: "The deployable unit is cipher plus mode plus nonce strategy plus authentication: ECB leaks repeated plaintext blocks and CBC alone adds no integrity, so the same AES can be safe or unsafe depending on the construction around it. Key length is one input to that decision rather than the whole of it, and a standard approves the primitive." },
  { ch: 4, type: "study", q: "Which AES round step is the only one that involves the secret key?", opts: ["SubBytes, because the substitution box is keyed", "ShiftRows, because the rotation amount is derived from the key", "MixColumns, because the polynomial is secret", "AddRoundKey, which XORs the state with the round keys produced by the key schedule"], answer: 3, exp: "SubBytes, ShiftRows and MixColumns are fixed public transformations of the state, which is what lets the whole specification be published; only AddRoundKey injects secret material. The substitution box and the MixColumns polynomial are public constants, and ShiftRows is a fixed rotation of row r by r bytes." },
  { ch: 4, type: "study", q: "A 4 MB file is encrypted with AES in ECB mode, and the ciphertext visibly contains the same 16-byte pattern at four separate offsets. What has gone wrong?", opts: ["The block size is too small, so patterns survive encryption", "The same key was reused for each of the four blocks", "The file was not padded to a multiple of the block size", "The mode, not the cipher: ECB encrypts blocks independently, so identical plaintext blocks become identical ciphertext blocks"], answer: 3, exp: "ECB publishes any repetition in the plaintext because each block is encrypted without reference to the others, which is why the deck treats it as the classic wrong choice. The 128-bit block size is the standard AES parameter and is not the defect, and a mode such as CBC or GCM under one key would have produced different ciphertext for identical blocks." },
  { ch: 4, type: "study", q: "Which combination of a goal and a primitive is correctly matched?", opts: ["Confidentiality of a 2 GB backup - a bare SHA-256 digest of the archive", "Detection of unauthorised change to web content by an attacker who can edit it - the bare SHA-256 digest published alongside the page", "Non-repudiation of a signed contract - a MAC using a key both parties hold", "Confidentiality and integrity of a 2 GB backup - an AEAD construction such as AES-GCM or ChaCha20-Poly1305"], answer: 3, exp: "An AEAD supplies encryption and a tag in one construction, which is exactly the pair of properties bulk-at-rest protection needs. A digest hides nothing, a bare digest is replaceable by an attacker who can edit the data, and a MAC cannot give non-repudiation because every key holder could have produced the tag." },
  { ch: 4, type: "study", q: "A service derives its AES key as the SHA-256 digest of a memorable phrase, and the key is 256 bits long. How strong is it?", opts: ["256 bits of security, because the digest is 256 bits long", "Secure, provided the hash function is an approved standard such as SHA-256", "Far weaker than 256 bits: the effective search space is the guessing space of the phrase, not the output length of the hash", "Weak only if the phrase is shorter than 16 characters"], answer: 2, exp: "Hashing spreads the bits it is given but cannot create unpredictability, so the work factor is bounded by the entropy of the phrase, which is far below the digest width. Key length sets the maximum search space only when every bit of the field can actually vary, and a digest of a phrase has as many distinguishable outputs as the phrase has plausible variants." },
  { ch: 4, type: "math", q: "A designer proposes AES-128 in GCM mode with a fresh nonce per message, and an alternative of AES-256 in ECB mode with a fixed nonce. Which is the better choice, and why?", opts: ["The 256-bit option, because a larger key is always stronger", "They are equivalent, because both use AES and both keep the nonce secret", "The 256-bit option, because ECB provides authentication that GCM lacks", "The 128-bit option: AES-128 already reaches the 128-bit security level, while ECB leaks patterns, carries no integrity and the fixed nonce breaks the AEAD requirement"], answer: 3, exp: "Security is bounded by the construction rather than the largest number in it: ECB reveals repeated plaintext blocks and has no tag, and a fixed nonce under GCM destroys both confidentiality and authentication. A 256-bit key does not repair a broken mode, and the two designs are not equivalent." },
  { ch: 4, type: "math", q: "A design uses SHA-1 to fingerprint a 1 GB software release and argues that a 160-bit digest is plenty because an attacker would need about 2 to the 160 work. What is wrong with the argument?", opts: ["Collision resistance costs about 2 to the 80 because of the birthday bound, and SHA-1 collisions have been demonstrated in practice", "SHA-1 outputs 128 bits rather than 160, so the work factor is even lower", "The digest must be shortened to raise the birthday bound", "A 1 GB input is too large for SHA-1 to process, so the digest is unreliable"], answer: 0, exp: "An attacker who needs any two colliding messages, rather than a match to a fixed digest, benefits from the birthday effect and pays about the square root of the digest space, which is why MD5 and SHA-1 fell first. SHA-1 really does output 160 bits, and shortening a digest lowers its collision resistance rather than raising it." },
  { ch: 4, type: "math", q: "A sender uses a one-time pad key twice. The two ciphertexts are XORed together by an observer. What does the observer hold, and what follows?", opts: ["The key, because it appears in both ciphertexts and cancels", "The XOR of the two plaintexts, because the key cancels out of the difference, which is enough to recover both with ordinary language statistics", "Half of each plaintext, with the remainder requiring a third reuse", "Nothing useful, because XOR of two ciphertexts is uniformly random"], answer: 1, exp: "Substituting the encryption equation gives C1 XOR C2 = M1 XOR M2, so the secret vanishes from the attacker's problem and what remains is a classical two-time-pad cryptogram. The key does not appear in the difference at all, which is the opposite of the first option, and the result is far from uniform when the plaintexts are natural language." },
  { ch: 4, type: "math", q: "A service encrypts with AES-128-GCM under one long-lived key and derives each record nonce as a 32-bit counter plus a fixed prefix. At roughly how many records does the GCM security argument break down, and why?", opts: ["At about 2 to the 32 records, because that is when the counter wraps", "At about 2 to the 128 records, because GCM has a 128-bit tag", "Never, because a counter can never repeat a value", "By about 2 to the 64 records, because the birthday bound over a 32-bit varying nonce makes a repeat likely long before the tag offers any protection"], answer: 3, exp: "Nonce uniqueness is the assumption GCM depends on, and a 32-bit varying field collides by the birthday bound after roughly the square root of the space, which is about 2 to the 32 - and TLS designs rekey well before that. The tag length governs forgery probability rather than nonce collision, and a counter does repeat once it wraps." },
  { ch: 4, type: "study", q: "A reviewer argues that the one-time pad should be deployed for a 1 GB nightly backup because it gives perfect secrecy. Which two conditions make that impractical?", opts: ["The key must be shorter than the message, and it must be stored in plaintext", "The ciphertext must be kept secret, and the key may be reused once", "The message must be compressed first, and the key must be derived from a password", "The key must be as long as the message and delivered over an already secure channel in advance, and it must never be reused"], answer: 3, exp: "Key length equal to message length plus advance delivery over a channel that is already secure is the cost the deck names, and reuse collapses the guarantee to the XOR of two plaintexts. Perfect secrecy is real but unusable at scale, which is why practical ciphers keep a short secret instead." },
  { ch: 4, type: "study", q: "Which statement about authenticated encryption is correct?", opts: ["GCM encrypts and authenticates in one construction, and verification failure means the plaintext must be rejected rather than processed", "GCM provides encryption only, and authentication must be added by a separate hash", "A tag that fails verification may still be processed if the ciphertext decrypts cleanly", "CBC with a random IV is an AEAD construction because the IV prevents repetition"], answer: 0, exp: "An AEAD returns ciphertext and a tag, and the receiver rejects the message when the tag does not verify, which is what makes tampering detectable. CBC hides repeated plaintext patterns but supplies confidentiality only, and a hash added separately is not an authenticated construction." },

  /* Ch5 */
  { ch: 5, type: "study", q: "Which statement about a hash function with an n-bit digest is correct?", opts: ["Preimage and collision resistance both cost about 2 to the n", "Collision resistance costs about 2 to the n/2, which is why SHA-1's 160-bit digest is only about 2 to the 80 against collisions", "Collision resistance is stronger than preimage resistance because two messages are involved", "Second-preimage resistance costs about 2 to the n/2 because the target message is known"], answer: 1, exp: "The birthday effect makes a collision the cheapest attack, at roughly the square root of the digest space, which is why a digest must be twice the intended security level. Preimage resistance is the expensive one at about 2 to the n, and knowing the first message does not give a second-preimage attack a birthday advantage." },
  { ch: 5, type: "study", q: "A vendor signs its installer with a private key and publishes the installer plus the signature. Which statement is correct?", opts: ["The vendor must distribute its private key so that users can verify the installer", "Signing encrypts the installer, so only holders of the public key can read it", "Users verify with the vendor's public key, and the installer itself remains readable to anyone", "Verification requires a shared secret that only the vendor and each user hold"], answer: 2, exp: "A signature gives integrity, origin authentication and public verifiability, so one private key signs and many public keys verify, and no secret is handed out. Signing hides nothing - the deck is explicit that encrypting with a private key is the wrong description - and a shared secret describes a MAC, which cannot support public verification at scale." },
  { ch: 5, type: "study", q: "An update design publishes a SHA-256 digest of the package on the same server as the package, so that clients can check it before installing. What is the flaw?", opts: ["SHA-256 is deprecated and should be replaced by SHA-1 for this purpose", "The digest must be encrypted with the server's public key before publication", "A digest cannot detect a single changed byte, so the check is useless", "An attacker who controls the download replaces the package and the digest together, so integrity needs a signature or a MAC"], answer: 3, exp: "A hash is unkeyed, so anyone who can modify the data can recompute the fingerprint, which is why the week says a bare hash is only a fingerprint. SHA-256 is the current default rather than deprecated, and the avalanche property means a single changed byte does change the digest." },
  { ch: 5, type: "study", q: "An engineer proposes protecting software updates with HMAC-SHA-256, using one key shared with every customer. What is wrong with the plan?", opts: ["HMAC-SHA-256 cannot detect modification, so it provides no integrity at all", "The tag length would be too short to resist forgery", "Any key holder can forge an update that every other user accepts, so the design needs signatures with one private signer and many public verifiers", "HMAC requires a certificate, which the plan does not mention"], answer: 2, exp: "A MAC proves authenticity only while the key stays secret from attackers, so a shared key turns every customer into a potential forger, which is the argument the week uses to motivate signatures. HMAC does detect modification, its tag can be the full digest length, and it needs a shared key rather than a certificate." },
  { ch: 5, type: "study", q: "A client validates a server certificate successfully and the chain builds to a trusted root. What has the client established?", opts: ["That the server holds a private key bound to the requested name by a chain from a trusted anchor - and nothing about whether the operator is honest", "That the operator of the site is trustworthy, because a CA verified the organisation", "That the session content will be confidential, because the certificate was valid", "That the server's software is free of vulnerabilities, because the CA performed a security review"], answer: 0, exp: "Certificates solve the binding problem: they connect a public key to a name through a trusted anchor, which is exactly what a valid signature alone cannot do. A valid certificate is compatible with a site that exists only to defraud, and neither confidentiality nor the absence of software flaws follows from the certificate." },
  { ch: 5, type: "math", q: "In an RSA key pair generated from p = 61 and q = 53 with public exponent e = 17, phi(n) works out to 3120. Which value must never be published, and why?", opts: ["The modulus n, because it is the product of the two primes", "phi(n), because with e it immediately yields the private exponent d", "The public exponent e, because it is the inverse of d", "The ciphertext c, because the message can be recovered from it by anyone"], answer: 1, exp: "The private exponent is the inverse of e modulo phi(n), so publishing phi(n) hands an attacker d in one extended-Euclidean step. The modulus is published deliberately as part of the public key, e is the public exponent, and the ciphertext is expected to travel in the open." },
  { ch: 5, type: "math", q: "Alice and Bob agree on the group p = 23, g = 5, choose a = 6 and b = 15, and exchange A = 8 and B = 19. What is the shared secret, and what does the exchange not provide?", opts: ["The shared secret is 8, and the exchange hides the public values from an observer", "The shared secret is 19, and the exchange proves Alice's identity to Bob", "The shared secret is 2, and the exchange provides no assurance about who is on the other end", "The shared secret is 5, and the exchange provides confidentiality against an active attacker"], answer: 2, exp: "Both sides compute 5 to the 90 modulo 23, and since 5 has order 22 in this group and 90 leaves remainder 2, the secret is 25 mod 23 = 2. The public values A, B, g and p are visible by design, and authentication is exactly what unauthenticated Diffie-Hellman omits, which is why an attacker can substitute both values." },
  { ch: 5, type: "math", q: "A client receives a certificate whose subject alternative names read DNS:*.example.com, and the user typed https://login.eu.example.com. What does a correct client do?", opts: ["Accept, because the wildcard covers every subdomain below example.com", "Reject, because the wildcard matches exactly one label and this name has two labels in the wildcard position", "Accept, provided the certificate is inside its validity window", "Accept, because the certificate chains to a trusted root"], answer: 1, exp: "A wildcard substitutes for a single label, so *.example.com matches www.example.com and not a name with two labels before the registered domain. Validity and chain building are separate conjuncts of the validation decision and neither repairs a name mismatch." },
  { ch: 5, type: "math", q: "A team needs 128-bit security and signs documents with RSA using SHA-256. Which statement about the choice is correct?", opts: ["It is inconsistent, because SHA-256 gives 256-bit security, which is more than required", "It is inconsistent, because a signature needs a hash with the same bit length as the modulus", "It is inconsistent, because SHA-256 provides no collision resistance at all", "It is consistent, because SHA-256 supplies about 128 bits of collision resistance, which matches the target level"], answer: 3, exp: "The birthday bound halves the digest width, so SHA-256 gives about 128 bits against collisions and matches a 128-bit target, which is why NIST pairs it with 3072-bit RSA at that level. A digest wider than necessary is not an inconsistency, and bit-length equality between hash and modulus is not a requirement." },
  { ch: 5, type: "math", q: "An HMAC-SHA-256 value is being computed over a 20-byte key. The underlying hash has a 64-byte block size. Which statement about the construction is correct?", opts: ["The key is used directly, because it is shorter than the block size", "The key is hashed first, because any key shorter than the block size must be compressed", "The key is truncated to 16 bytes to match the tag length", "The key is padded with zero bytes to the 64-byte block size before the inner and outer padding are applied"], answer: 3, exp: "HMAC normalises the key to one hash block: longer keys are hashed and then padded, shorter keys are zero-padded, and the result is XORed with the inner and outer padding constants. Hashing a short key, truncating it to the tag length, or using it unpadded are all variations that break the FIPS 198-1 construction." },

  /* Ch6 */
  { ch: 6, type: "study", q: "A university database is encrypted with TLS in front of it, yet 8,000 student records are exposed after an attacker logs in with a valid staff password. Which explanation is correct?", opts: ["Encryption at rest failed, because a well-configured store never returns plaintext", "The key length was too short, so the ciphertext could be decrypted offline", "The attacker held a valid credential, so the session was authorised and the application returned readable records", "TLS terminated at the load balancer, leaving the last hop unencrypted"], answer: 3, exp: "An application must decrypt the data it serves, so a legitimate session receives plaintext and the failure lives in authentication, authorisation or detection rather than in cryptography. Key length and TLS termination are encryption stories that do not explain a successful login with a genuine password." },
  { ch: 6, type: "study", q: "A portal requires a password and then a PIN that the same user memorised. How should this be described?", opts: ["Two-factor authentication, because two separate secrets are required", "Two authentication steps inside one factor category, so the independent-evidence property is not met", "Three-factor authentication, because the PIN is verified by a different component", "Strong authentication, because a PIN has fewer bits than a password and is therefore harder"], answer: 1, exp: "Factors are counted by category and each must be independently verified, so two things a user knows are two steps and one factor, which is the counting rule the deck stresses. Which component verifies the value does not change its category, and a short numeric secret is weaker rather than stronger." },
  { ch: 6, type: "study", q: "A logged-in student requests another student's mark record by editing the record identifier in the URL. The server returns it. Which layer failed?", opts: ["Authentication, because the attacker should not have been able to log in", "Encryption, because record identifiers should not appear in URLs", "Authorisation, specifically object-level authorisation, because the server never checked whether this subject may reach that object", "Availability, because the record was reachable when it should not have been"], answer: 2, exp: "The session was valid, so authentication succeeded at the only question it was asked; the missing decision is whether this subject may act on this object, which is object-level authorisation. Encrypting an identifier obscures the request without adding a permission check, and availability is unrelated." },
  { ch: 6, type: "study", q: "Administrators are issued push-approval prompts for every login, and one administrator approves an unexpected prompt at 2 a.m. Which control change addresses this specific failure?", opts: ["Lengthen the administrator password and force rotation every 60 days", "Add a second push prompt so the administrator is asked twice", "Disable multi-factor authentication for administrators to remove the fatigue", "Move administrators to phishing-resistant credentials such as a security key or passkey, with number matching as a minimum"], answer: 3, exp: "Push fatigue recruits the user rather than defeating the factor, and the deck's remedy is a credential the user cannot be talked into approving, with number matching as the floor. A longer password does not help because the password was not the weak point, and asking twice multiplies the fatigue rather than removing it." },
  { ch: 6, type: "study", q: "A help desk resets accounts after the caller gives a name and date of birth. Why does the deck treat this as a first-order problem?", opts: ["Because recovery is an authentication path, and a weak fallback bypasses the strongest primary method in the system", "Because resets are expensive and should be automated instead", "Because name and date of birth are protected personal information", "Because the help desk cannot log the reset in the audit trail"], answer: 0, exp: "Principle five is that recovery must be secure, for the reason the 2:13 a.m. scenario shows: an attacker who cannot defeat the primary method simply uses the fallback. Cost, data classification and logging are real considerations but none of them is why the fallback determines the security of the whole design." },
  { ch: 6, type: "study", q: "In a TLS 1.3 handshake, which element can a passive network observer read directly from the wire?", opts: ["The server's certificate and its public key", "The plaintext of the first application request", "The transcripts covered by the Finished messages", "The server name in the ClientHello and the cipher suites the client offers"], answer: 3, exp: "The ClientHello and ServerHello travel in clear, so the server name and the offered suites are visible while the certificate, extensions and application data sit inside the encrypted envelope. The certificate is sent after handshake keys exist, application keys are derived later still, and Finished covers a transcript that is itself encrypted." },
  { ch: 6, type: "math", q: "A password is 10 characters drawn uniformly from the 62 letters-and-digits characters. Roughly how much entropy does it carry?", opts: ["About 10 bits, one for each character", "About 59.5 bits, from 10 times log base 2 of 62", "About 62 bits, because there are 62 possible characters", "About 36 bits, because only the length matters"], answer: 1, exp: "Entropy is length times the base-2 logarithm of the alphabet size, and log base 2 of 62 is about 5.95, so ten characters give about 59.5 bits. One bit per character understates a 62-symbol alphabet, and quoting the alphabet size as the entropy confuses the number of symbols with the size of the space." },
  { ch: 6, type: "math", q: "A verifier database of 50,000 rows leaks. Every row has a 16-byte per-account salt and Argon2id parameters of m = 64 MiB, t = 3, p = 1. Which statement is correct?", opts: ["Each guess is expensive, but the attack is offline with no rate limit, so weak passwords still fall", "The salts make the whole database computationally infeasible to attack", "The leak is contained as long as the salts themselves were kept secret", "The memory cost only slows the defender's login and does not affect the attacker"], answer: 0, exp: "A salt destroys precomputed tables and stops two identical passwords sharing a verifier, but it does not slow a single guess, and the attacker's cost per guess is what the memory-time parameters raise. Salts are stored beside the verifier by design, so they were never secret, and the work factor applies to attacker and defender alike." },
  { ch: 6, type: "math", q: "A portal verifies a password and then a one-time code, both typed into the same page, and the page is served from the real domain. Which description of the resulting strength is correct?", opts: ["Genuine two-factor strength, because a stolen password alone is useless", "A single factor entered twice, exactly like a password plus a PIN", "Real protection against password theft, but not a product of two probabilities if one real-time phishing page can relay both", "Fully independent, because the password and the code use unrelated algorithms"], answer: 2, exp: "The factors are in different categories, so this is real multi-factor authentication against a stolen password; what it is not is a product of two bypass probabilities when a single page can relay both values live. The factors are not the same category, so it is not password plus PIN, and using different algorithms does not make the verification channels independent." },
  { ch: 6, type: "math", q: "An organisation has 50,000 staff accounts, applies a five-attempt per-account lockout, and an attacker runs one password per account from a breach list, succeeding on 0.1% of accounts. What is the expected outcome and the correct control?", opts: ["Lockout blocks the attack, because five attempts per account never exceeds the threshold", "About 50 accounts are compromised; per-account lockout does not bound a horizontal attack, so population-level rate limiting, breach-list checks and MFA are needed", "About 5,000 accounts are compromised, since 0.1% of the population falls to any single attempt", "No accounts are compromised, because each account sees only one failed attempt"], answer: 1, exp: "Fifty thousand accounts at a 0.1% success rate gives about 50 compromised accounts while each individual account stays under the lockout threshold, which is precisely why password spraying evades per-account limits. The expected number follows from multiplying population by success rate, and lockout is a per-account bound rather than a population one." },
  { ch: 6, type: "math", q: "An application derives its data key by hashing a memorable phrase with SHA-256, and the resulting key is 256 bits long. A reviewer claims this delivers 256-bit strength because that is the digest width. Why is the claim wrong?", opts: ["Because SHA-256 is not collision resistant and should not be used for keys", "Because the effective search space is the number of plausible phrases, so the strength is bounded by the phrase's entropy rather than by the digest width", "Because hashing a phrase produces fewer than 256 bits of usable output", "Because a 256-bit key must be generated by an approved key-derivation function with a salt"], answer: 1, exp: "The work factor cannot exceed the number of distinguishable inputs, so a digest of a guessable phrase is guessable at the phrase's own cost, which the week writes as work bounded by two to the entropy. The digest really is 256 bits wide and SHA-256 is collision resistant; width is simply not the same quantity as entropy." },

  /* Ch7 */
  { ch: 7, type: "study", q: "A voice call keeps going when some packets are dropped, while a file download stalls and then recovers. Which transport property explains the difference?", opts: ["The voice call uses encryption and the download does not", "UDP leaves loss to the application, while TCP acknowledges and retransmits to deliver a complete ordered stream", "UDP guarantees ordering and TCP does not", "The voice call uses IP directly and the download uses UDP"], answer: 1, exp: "UDP sends independent datagrams with no ordering or retransmission, which suits data whose value decays with time, while TCP rebuilds an ordered stream by acknowledging the next expected byte and resending what is missing. Neither protocol supplies secrecy, and IP is the best-effort layer beneath both." },
  { ch: 7, type: "study", q: "A SYN flood is running against a web server that is still serving cached pages to existing sessions. Which property is under attack, and what is the impact?", opts: ["Confidentiality, because the attacker is reading handshake traffic", "Integrity, because the attacker is replaying completed handshakes", "Availability: half-open entries fill the backlog, so legitimate clients cannot connect", "Reliability of delivery, because retransmissions overwhelm the network"], answer: 2, exp: "The attacker asks the server to allocate state by starting handshakes it never finishes, so the resource that runs out is the backlog and the service degrades for new legitimate clients. Nothing is read or altered, which rules out confidentiality and integrity, and TCP reliability is the mechanism being exhausted rather than the property attacked." },
  { ch: 7, type: "study", q: "A user is sent to paypa1-secure.example.net, which presents a genuine, currently valid certificate issued for exactly that name. Why does this still fail to protect the user?", opts: ["The certificate is self-signed and therefore untrusted", "PKI binds a key to a name, not to intent - every technical check legitimately passes for a name the attacker owns", "The redirect downgraded the connection to plaintext HTTP", "The certificate must have expired, since the browser accepted it"], answer: 1, exp: "A certificate proves that some name is bound to some key, and a hostname check proves the name is the one requested, which an attacker-controlled look-alike domain satisfies honestly. The deck's point is that the technical checks pass for a domain the attacker legitimately controls, so the defence is user-side verification rather than PKI." },
  { ch: 7, type: "study", q: "A service serves HTTPS correctly but never sends an HSTS header, and its plain HTTP port stays open with a same-host redirect. Which attack remains available, and to whom?", opts: ["SSL stripping on the first visit, to an attacker already on the path", "Recovery of the session keys from a later server compromise", "Decryption of the TLS records by a passive observer of the encrypted session", "Replay of application records by an observer who cannot modify them"], answer: 0, exp: "Without a long-lived HSTS policy the browser has no instruction to upgrade before the first request leaves the device, so an attacker in the path can answer that plaintext request and keep the session in the clear. Forward secrecy prevents the later-compromise attack, record encryption prevents passive reading, and the record tag prevents silent replay." },
  { ch: 7, type: "math", q: "A full-size TLS 1.3 record on an Ethernet path carries 1438 application bytes inside a 1514-byte frame. About what fraction of the bytes on the wire is application data, and what does the gap cost?", opts: ["About 95%: the 22-byte TLS overhead costs roughly 1.5 percentage points against the same frame without TLS", "About 1.5%, because the record header dominates a full-size frame", "About 50%, because encryption doubles the bytes on the wire", "About 76%, because the IP and TCP headers are counted twice"], answer: 0, exp: "Efficiency is payload over wire length, so 1438 divided by 1514 is about 0.95, and the unprotected comparison 1460 divided by 1514 shows the protection costs about 1.5 percentage points. The 22-byte record overhead is a small fraction of a full-size frame, and encryption does not double anything." },
  { ch: 7, type: "math", q: "A path has 200 Mbit/s capacity and a 100 ms round-trip time, and the peer advertises an unscaled 16-bit window. What follows?", opts: ["The window is sufficient, because 65,535 bytes always exceeds any bandwidth-delay product", "The window must be exactly 65,535 bytes to avoid congestion", "The bandwidth-delay product is 2.5 MB, so the unscaled window cannot keep the pipe full and window scaling is required", "Throughput depends only on link capacity, so the window is irrelevant"], answer: 2, exp: "Throughput is bounded by window divided by round-trip time, and the capacity-delay product here is 200 million bits per second times 0.1 s, which is 2.5 MB - far above the 65,535-byte unscaled maximum. Window scaling exists precisely to lift that ceiling, and capacity alone does not determine achievable throughput on a long path." },
  { ch: 7, type: "math", q: "A deployment is described as using AES-256 and therefore offering 256-bit security. Where is the reasoning incomplete?", opts: ["AES-256 offers less than 256-bit security against exhaustive key search", "Symmetric key size never determines security strength", "The claim is wrong only if the record tag is shorter than 128 bits", "The overall strength is bounded by the weakest component - a 2048-bit RSA signature is about 112 bits - and by correct nonce handling"], answer: 3, exp: "Security is a minimum over the components and over the discipline in using them, so a 256-bit record key does not lift a 112-bit authentication step or repair a repeated nonce. A 256-bit symmetric key does resist exhaustive search at about 2 to the 256, and nonce misuse breaks GCM regardless of key length." },
  { ch: 7, type: "math", q: "A client receives a chain of three certificates and a leaf whose SAN list reads DNS:*.shop.example, DNS:shop.example. The user typed https://checkout.shop.example. What is the outcome?", opts: ["Reject: the wildcard matches only one label, and this host has two labels before the domain", "Accept: the wildcard matches the host, and the remaining predicates are chain signatures, validity windows, key usage and revocation status", "Accept on the name alone, because the name check replaces the chain check", "Reject: a certificate may list only one subject alternative name"], answer: 1, exp: "The requested host has exactly one label in the wildcard position, so it matches, and validity then depends on the conjunction of the remaining predicates: every link signed, every certificate inside its window, the right extended key usage and a revocation answer. Name matching is one conjunct rather than the whole decision, and a SAN list may hold several names." },
  { ch: 7, type: "math", q: "A capture shows ClientHello offering TLS_AES_128_GCM_SHA256 with an X25519 key share, then ServerHello selecting the same suite, then encrypted handshake messages including CertificateVerify. Which element turns the anonymous key agreement into an authenticated one?", opts: ["The cipher suite name, because it determines the server's signature strength", "The key share itself, because X25519 authenticates the peer", "The Finished message, because it confirms the transcript was unchanged", "The signature over the transcript hash in CertificateVerify, made with the key matching the certificate"], answer: 3, exp: "Ephemeral Diffie-Hellman agrees a secret with anybody, so authenticity comes from the signature over the transcript verified against the certified public key, which binds the exchange to the name the client validated. The cipher suite names record protection and a hash rather than the signature strength, and Finished proves integrity of the negotiation rather than identity." },
  { ch: 7, type: "math", q: "A site sets Strict-Transport-Security: max-age=86400 with neither includeSubDomains nor preload. What is the practical consequence?", opts: ["Browsers ignore the header entirely because one day is below the minimum", "The header removes the need for certificate validation during that day", "The browser enforces HTTPS for one day on that host only, so protection expires quickly and the first-visit window stays open on every other subdomain and every new profile", "The header protects every subdomain for a year, since max-age is measured in days"], answer: 2, exp: "The max-age value is a duration in seconds during which that host is treated as HTTPS-only, so 86,400 seconds is one day and the policy covers no subdomains without includeSubDomains and no first visit without preload. HSTS never replaces certificate validation, and the preload-list threshold of one year is a listing requirement rather than how max-age is interpreted." },

  /* Ch8 */
  { ch: 8, type: "study", q: "Which pair of definitions matches the Week 8 vocabulary?", opts: ["A vulnerability is an attacker's capability, and an exploit is the damage it causes", "A vulnerability is a missing patch, and an exploit is a scanner that finds it", "A vulnerability is any bug, and an exploit is any unexpected behaviour", "A vulnerability is a weakness that can be used to violate a security property, and an exploit is the means of using it"], answer: 3, exp: "The weakness lives in the application and the exploit is the means of using it, while the threat is the actor or event and the impact is the consequence. Confusing the weakness with the actor, the tool or the consequence is the definitional error the Foundations page warns about." },
  { ch: 8, type: "study", q: "Management asks which layer can tell them that an attack is happening right now, rather than whether the code could be attacked. What is the correct answer?", opts: ["Secure coding, because well-written code cannot be attacked", "Monitoring with IDS or IPS: the run-time half of the control set", "Static analysis, because it reads every line of the codebase", "Vulnerability scanning, because it enumerates every weakness"], answer: 1, exp: "Monitoring supplies the evidence that something happened and an IDS or IPS alerts or blocks on it, which is the run-time half of the deck's layered bar. Secure coding, static analysis and scanning are build-time controls: they change the probability that a weakness exists rather than telling you that it is being used." },
  { ch: 8, type: "study", q: "A service ships with the database vendor's sample administrator account still enabled and reachable from the internet. In the deck's lifecycle vocabulary, which defect is this?", opts: ["Unsafe data handling at the implementation stage", "Patch delayed at the maintenance stage", "A missed trust boundary at the design stage", "Insecure default at the deployment stage"], answer: 3, exp: "The defect is a capability that ships enabled because it was convenient in development, which is the deployment stage's characteristic failure. Implementation refers to how data is handled, maintenance to how promptly known flaws are fixed, and design to where trust boundaries and privilege are placed." },
  { ch: 8, type: "study", q: "What distinguishes an inference attack from a direct database attack such as SQL injection?", opts: ["Inference needs a vulnerability in the query builder, while injection does not", "Inference is a network-layer attack, while injection is an application-layer attack", "Inference is always carried out by an insider, while injection is always remote", "Inference bypasses no access control: it derives sensitive information by combining answers to queries the system permits"], answer: 3, exp: "The deck splits the two families by mechanism: direct attacks manipulate the query through unsafe input handling, while indirect attacks learn from what permitted answers reveal, which is why the mitigation is about what the system is willing to answer. Both can be run remotely or by an insider, and inference needs no code defect at all." },
  { ch: 8, type: "study", q: "A team blocks single quotes and the word OR at the input boundary and calls the injection risk closed. Why is this not a fix?", opts: ["Because input filtering slows the request path noticeably", "Because denylists are incomplete and bypassable, while parameter binding makes the statement structure independent of input", "Because the correct fix is to hide database error messages from users", "Because injection can only be prevented inside the database engine"], answer: 1, exp: "Validation constrains what a field may contain but cannot make an assembled string safe, and any path that reaches the same query builder without the filter reintroduces the flaw, which is why the structural control is prepared statements with bound parameters. Hiding errors reduces information leakage rather than removing the injection, and the engine faithfully executes whatever statement it is given." },
  { ch: 8, type: "study", q: "A search field concatenates the user's value into a SQL string. Which statement is correct?", opts: ["The database is at fault, because it should reject malformed statements", "The web application firewall is the structural fix and the code can stay as it is", "The statement is a function of user input, so the fix is parameter binding that keeps the SQL structure constant and the value separate", "Escaping every quote by hand is equivalent to parameter binding"], answer: 2, exp: "The defect is that user text is interpreted as SQL syntax, so the value can change the statement's grammar, and binding removes that possibility by fixing the structure. The database executes what it is given, a WAF is an additional layer rather than the fix, and hand escaping varies by context and is famously incomplete." },
  { ch: 8, type: "study", q: "A stored comment containing a script element executes when other users open the page. Which control actually stops it?", opts: ["Rejecting any input that contains the word script", "Contextual output encoding through an auto-escaping template, with a sanitizer only where rich text is genuinely required", "Shortening session lifetimes to a few minutes", "Moving the comment storage to a separate database"], answer: 1, exp: "XSS is defeated where the data becomes markup rather than where it arrives, so encoding in the right output context makes the injected text render instead of execute. Denylists miss encodings and alternative syntaxes, short sessions limit the value of a stolen cookie without preventing execution, and storage location changes nothing about what the browser receives." },
  { ch: 8, type: "study", q: "An IDS reports 2,000 failed logins from 300 source addresses and the attack is still succeeding. What is the correct conclusion?", opts: ["The IDS is misconfigured, because it should have blocked the sources", "Signature databases cannot detect brute force, so nothing could have been done", "Detection worked and prevention was missing: an IDS is passive by design, so blocking needs an inline IPS or controls on the login path", "The authentication path is rate limited, so the logins must be legitimate"], answer: 2, exp: "An IDS sits on a monitoring path and alerts, which is exactly what happened, and stopping the attempts requires an inline control or authentication hardening such as rate limiting, lockout with backoff or multi-factor authentication. Brute force is detectable by rate-based rules, and the logins are demonstrably not legitimate." },
  { ch: 8, type: "math", q: "An exploit is public on day 0 and the affected hosts are patched on day 30. If an exposed host is compromised with daily probability 0.01, roughly what is the probability it is compromised during the window?", opts: ["About 26%, from one minus 0.99 to the thirtieth power", "About 30%, because the window is thirty days", "About 1%, because the daily rate is one percent", "About 74%, because that is the survival probability"], answer: 0, exp: "The chance of surviving the whole window is 0.99 to the 30th, about 0.74, so the compromise probability is about 0.26, and cutting the window to seven days would bring it near 7%. Multiplying the daily rate by the number of days adds rather than compounds, and the survival figure is the complement of the answer." },
  { ch: 8, type: "math", q: "An internal audit finding and an internet-facing finding carry the same severity score. The public one has exploit code available; the internal one does not. Which should be remediated first, and why?", opts: ["The internal one, because internal systems are monitored less often", "The public one, because exploitability and exposure dominate when severity is equal", "Whichever is quicker to close, so the closure rate stays high", "Neither, because equal severity means equal priority"], answer: 1, exp: "The prioritisation score multiplies severity by exposure and exploitability, so an internet-facing service with public exploit code is reachable by automated attackers today while the internal host is not. Ticket closure rate is an activity measure that improves when easy findings are closed, which is precisely why the week prefers exposure measures." },
  { ch: 8, type: "math", q: "A detector has a 99% detection rate and a 1% false-positive rate on a network where 1 event in 100,000 is a real attack. What should you expect from the alert queue?", opts: ["Roughly 99% of alerts will be real, matching the detection rate", "Almost every alert will be a false positive, because the base rate is tiny", "About half the alerts will be real, since sensitivity and specificity are similar", "Alert volume is unaffected by the base rate"], answer: 1, exp: "Bayes gives 0.99 times 0.00001 divided by that same term plus 0.01 times 0.99999, which is far below one percent precision, so the queue is almost entirely noise even though the detector is good. Quoting the detection rate as precision is the base-rate fallacy, and the base rate is the dominant term whenever events are overwhelmingly benign." },
  { ch: 8, type: "math", q: "A published chart shows 200 reviewers' progress lines without names, per-paper review counts and a daily time series. Which model explains why this is a privacy problem even though no individual value is shown?", opts: ["A cryptographic failure in the reporting pipeline", "Information accumulates: each harmless signal halves the field, so the anonymity set shrinks below the intended size", "A denial-of-service risk from publishing aggregate load", "A backup failure, because the source data was not retained"], answer: 1, exp: "Each signal contributes bits - counts narrow which reviewers were plausible, behavioural patterns distinguish individuals - and the leakage adds, so an intended anonymity set of 200 can fall to a handful of people. The problem is disclosure through permitted answers rather than a cryptographic or availability defect." },

];